  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Thursday: An upper level low is making its \nway inland off the PNW coast. A surface low is also ushering in \na cold front. Lift and instability associated with both the \nupper level and surface lows has resulted in isolated convection\nmoving through the forecast area. DCAPE values of 1000 J/kg \ncombined with a very dry boundary layer will result in main \nhazards being gusty outflow winds up to 40 mph and dry \nlightning. As the low moves further inland, it will track \nnortheastward through Washington and Idaho. Widespread rain \nshowers will move through the area later today, bringing \nbeneficial rains. Much of the forecast area is expected to see \n0.10 inches of rain or more through early Thursday morning. \nAreas that will see the highest rainfall amounts will be the \nmountains, which could see up to an inch of rain. \n\nAs the low passes through, the pressure gradient across the \nCascades will increase, bringing strong westerly winds across \nmost of the forecast area. Winds will increase late Wednesday \nmorning and continue through Thursday morning, though the peak \nof the winds will happen late Wednesday morning through \nafternoon. NBM shows maximum wind gusts 40-45 mph for many \nplaces, and up to 55 mph in the Blue Mountains. With these \nmagnitudes, a Wind Advisory has been issued from early this \nmorning through 11pm tonight from the Wenatchee and Waterville \nPlateau area eastward, through the Idaho Panhandle. A High Wind \nWarning has been issued for the Blue Mountains. Impacts from \nthese winds are blowing unsecured objects, tree limbs, the \npossibility for a few power outages, and difficult driving \nconditions, especially for high profile vehicles. \n\nFriday through Sunday: A broad upper trough will move through \nthe area Saturday, bringing further chances for precipitation. \nThis system will arrive with anomalously cold 850mb \ntemperatures, nearly 4-7 degrees colder than this time of year. \nAs the low tracks through the area, it will bring showers, \nespecially to the northern Idaho Panhandle and the Cascades. The\n25th-75th percentile for snow along the Cascades is 1-4 inches.\nThe basin and lowlands will mostly be dry. However, these \ncolder conditions bring the potential for frost issues, \nparticularly Saturday evening into Sunday morning, where there \nis a 40% chance and above for many areas to see low temperatures\nless than 36 degrees. /AS",
